<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.8.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00493</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.8.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>355c242f-9e3d-4271-a638-e0fc855e50f7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>For 1 - 2, use the following passage:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>A runner is training for a marathon. She runs more than 4 days each week. Each day, </para>
</blockquote>
<para>1. What does the inequality \(d &gt; 4\) represent?</para>
<list>
  <item>the number of miles in a marathon</item>
  <item>the number of miles the runner runs each day</item>
  <item>the number of months away the marathon is</item>
  <item>the number of days the runner runs each week</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> the number of days the runner runs each week </para>
<para>2. What does the inequality \(12 ≤ m ≤ 15\) represent? </para>
<list>
  <item>the number of miles in a marathon</item>
  <item>the number of miles the runner runs each day</item>
  <item>the number of months away the marathon is</item>
  <item>the number of days the runner runs each week</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> the number of miles the runner runs each day </para>
<para>For 3 - 8, write an inequality to represent the constraint given. </para>
<para>3. There are less than 4 patients, \(p\), in the waiting room.</para>
<list>
  <item>\(p &lt; 4 \)</item>
  <item>\(p &gt; 4 \)</item>
  <item>\(p ≤ 4 \)</item>
  <item>\(p ≥ 4 \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(p &lt; 4 \)</para>
<para>4. There are no more than 6 cats, \(c\), in the yard.</para>
<list>
  <item>\(c &gt; 6 \)</item>
  <item>\(c ≤ 6\)</item>
  <item>\(c &lt; 6 \)</item>
  <item>\(c ≥ 6 \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> c ≤ 6 </para>
<para>5. There are no less than 7 markers, \(m\), in the drawer.</para>
<list>
  <item>\(m &gt; 7 \)</item>
  <item>\(m ≤ 7\)</item>
  <item>\(m &lt; 7 \)</item>
  <item>\(m ≥ \)7 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> m ≥ 7 </para>
<para>6. The basket has between 12 and 19 eggs, \(e\).</para>
<list>
  <item>\(12 &gt; e\) and \(e &gt; 19 \)</item>
  <item>\(12 ≥ e\) and \(e ≥ 19 \)</item>
  <item>\(e ≤ 19 \)</item>
  <item>\(12 ≤ e ≤ 19 \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(12 ≤ e ≤ 19 \) </para>
<para>7. The total sales of tickets to a show is more than $68. An adult ticket, <emphasis effect="italics">a</emphasis>, costs $8 and a children’s ticket, \(c\), is $3.</para>
<list>
  <item>\( 8a + 3c ≤ 68 \)</item>
  <item>\( 8a + 3c ≥ 68 \)</item>
  <item>\( 8a + 3c &lt; 68 \)</item>
  <item>\( 8a + 3c &gt; 68 \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( 8a + 3c &gt; 68 \)<emphasis> </emphasis></para>
<para>8. There needs to be at least 1 pound of clay, \(c\), for every 4 students, \(s\).</para>
<list>
  <item>\( p ≥ \frac s4 \)</item>
  <item>\( p &gt; \frac s4 \)</item>
  <item>\( p &lt; \frac s4\)</item>
  <item>\( p ≤ \frac s4\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( p ≥ \frac s4 \)</para>
<para>For 9 - 11, use the following passage:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>A doctor sees between 7 and 12 patients each day. On Mondays and Tuesdays, the</para>
</blockquote>
<para>\(0.25 ≤ y ≤ 1\)                 \(7 ≤ x ≤ 12\)                   \( xy ≤ 8 \)</para>
<para>9. What does the variable <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis> represent?</para>
<list>
  <item>the number of patients the doctor sees each day</item>
  <item>the part of an hour that each appointment lasts</item>
  <item>the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day.</item>
  <item>the number of patients the doctor sees in a week</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> the number of patients the doctor sees each day</para>
<para>10. What does the variable <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis> represent?</para>
<list>
  <item>the number of patients the doctor sees each day</item>
  <item>the part of an hour that each appointment lasts</item>
  <item>the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day.</item>
  <item>the number of patients the doctor sees in a week</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> the part of an hour that each appointment lasts</para>
<para>11. What does the expression \(xy\) in the last inequality mean in this situation?</para>
<list>
  <item>the number of patients the doctor sees each day</item>
  <item>the part of an hour that each appointment lasts</item>
  <item>the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day.</item>
  <item>the number of patients the doctor sees in a week</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day</para>
<para>For 12 - 14, use the following passage:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Arnav wants to build a dog house. He makes a list of the materials needed:</para>

<list>
  <item>At least 60 square feet of plywood for the surfaces</item>
  <item>At least 36 feet of wood planks for the frame of the dog house </item>
  <item>Between 1 and 2 quarts of paint</item>
</list>
<para>Arnav's budget is $65. Plywood costs $0.70 per square foot, planks of wood cost $0.10 per foot, and paint costs $8 per quart.</para>
</blockquote>
  <para>12. Which inequality represents the square feet of plywood needed?</para>
  <list>
    <item>\(p ≥ 60 \)</item>
    <item>\(p ≥ 36 \)</item>
    <item>\(1 ≤ p ≤ 2\)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(p ≥ 60 \)</para>
  <para>13. Which inequality represents the number of wood planks needed?</para>
  <list>
    <item>\(w ≥ 60 \)</item>
    <item>\(1 ≤ w ≤ 2\)</item>
    <item>\(w ≥ 36 \)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(w ≥ 36 \)</para>
  <para>14. Which inequality represents the number of quarts of paint needed?</para>
  <list>
    <item>\(q ≥ 60 \)</item>
    <item>\(1 ≤ q ≤ 2\)</item>
    <item>\(q ≥ 36 \)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(1 ≤ q ≤ 2\)</para>

</section>
